I'm wondering....

I've often considered the mazes to be the most boring part of the game
as well, and I'm starting to consider if the problem might be due to
the number of items in the game....

As it is, I'd say there's a good chance that, by the time the player
gets past the Castle and the Valley of the Dead, that he's seen at
least two-thirds of the "normal," that is non-artifact,
randomly-generated items in the game.  By this time, if he's played
his cards right, he has a luckstone and either an amulet of reflection
or a bag of holding, and a wand of wishing.  That wand of wishing
enables the player to fill almost any ascension kit inventory hole he
has remaining.

Nethack is a game of discovery, and most of the time the things that
need discovering are items, both to collect and to identify.  The
right items can give even a low-level character a chance at ascension,
while an under-equipped level 30 character will have big problems long
before the endgame.  Finding the right item early can flavor the
entire game -- an early cloak of displacement, wand of death, scroll
of genocide, artifact weapon, gauntlets of power, etc.  That's why I
enjoy playing wizards: they start with random nifty stuff.

Furthermore, many of the most essential intrinsics have multiple
sources.  Let's see: the player needs a source of Magic Resistance to
avoid touch-of-death (GDSM, cloak, Magicbane, some quest artifacts),
Fire Resistance to deal with the fire traps in Gehennom (ring, RDSM,
eating), and Levitation to get past Medusa, and to help with Jubilex
(boots, ring, scroll).  Reflection helps with zappers & dragons, or
you can just be immune to most of the things you can get zapped with. 
The worst things you can get zapped with in the late game - Fire, Ice,
Lightning, Disintegration - are dangerous more for their
item-destroying effects than HP damage.

Submitted for debate: I present the theory that Nethack gets boring
later on, not just because the mazes are a pain to get through (and
they are), but because magic items are either subtlely a little too
common relative to the size of the game, or the wand of wishing in the
Castle is too useful.

